{
  "manufacturer": "Schneider Electric",
  "manufacturer_slug": "schneider-electric",
  "model": "PowerTag Energy wireless sensors via PowerTag Link gateway (Modbus TCP)",
  "model_slug": "powertag-energy-powertag-link",
  "short_model": "PowerTag Link",
  "aliases": [
    "PowerTag Link",
    "PowerTag Link HD",
    "A9XMWD20",
    "A9XMWD100",
    "PowerTag Energy 63",
    "A9MEM1520",
    "A9MEM1540",
    "A9MEM1560",
    "A9MEM1570",
    "PowerTag M250",
    "PowerTag M630",
    "LV434020",
    "PowerTag F160",
    "A9MEM1580",
    "PowerTag Rope",
    "A9MEM1590"
  ],
  "equipment_type": "Energy meter (wireless sensors through an Ethernet gateway)",
  "applies_to": "PowerTag Energy 63, M250/M630, F160 and Rope sensors read through a PowerTag Link or PowerTag Link HD gateway, per DOCA0157EN-09 (10/2025).",
  "summary": "PowerTag Energy sensors clip onto breakers and report over radio to a PowerTag Link gateway, which serves each sensor as its own Modbus TCP server address (1-247). Every sensor exposes the same register block: currents, voltages, power and power factor as big-endian floats, and energy counters as 64-bit integers in Wh. Which registers a sensor fills depends on its type (63 A, M250/M630, F160/Rope).",
  "transport": [
    "TCP"
  ],
  "defaults": {
    "unit_id": null,
    "baud": null,
    "parity": null,
    "data_bits": null,
    "stop_bits": null,
    "tcp_port": 502
  },
  "addressing": {
    "convention": "1-based",
    "note": "The guide prints register numbers in decimal and addresses in hex, with address = register - 1 (register 3000 is address 0x0BB7). Two hex cells in the table are misprinted (register 3228 shown as 0x09CB, register 5193 as 0x1488); wire addresses here follow the register-minus-one rule."
  },
  "default_word_order": "ABCD",
  "firmware": null,
  "source": {
    "title": "PowerTag Link Gateway User Guide",
    "doc_number": "DOCA0157EN-09",
    "revision_date": "2025-10",
    "url": "https://download.se.com/files?p_Doc_Ref=DOCA0157EN",
    "pages": "Data types and big-endian rule p. 93; frame example and addressing pp. 94-95; gateway registers pp. 96-97; PowerTag Energy sensor registers pp. 98-105"
  },
  "notes": [
    "The guide says 32-bit and 64-bit values are big-endian: most significant word first.",
    "Read a sensor by using its Modbus server address (set per wireless device on the gateway web pages, 1-247). The gateway's own identification registers answer on server address 255.",
    "Invalid or unavailable values read 0xFFC00000 (floats) or 0x8000000000000000 (64-bit energy).",
    "Each register's description says which sensor types fill it: PowerTag 63 = A9MEM152x/154x/156x/157x, M250/M630 = LV43402x, F160/Rope = A9MEM1580/A9MEM159x.",
    "Left out: the 'set partial counter' write registers (3260, 3268, 3276, 3284, 3292), demand timestamps (DATETIME), the PowerTag control-module, HeatTag, load-monitoring, configuration, diagnostic and synthesis-table registers.",
    "Schneider's function code 100-4 (read scattered registers) is also accepted for most float registers; this map uses plain function 03."
  ],
